<h1>Relative Athletic <em>Score (RAS)</em> Explained</h1>
<div class="hero-meta">
<span class="hero-meta-item">📖 9 min read</span>
<span class="hero-meta-item">🏈 NFL Draft</span>
<span class="hero-meta-item">📊 Combine Analytics</span>
</div>
<p class="hero-intro">
    Relative Athletic Score is one of the most useful tools in NFL draft analysis — and one of the most misunderstood. Here's exactly how it works, what scores mean at every position, the best and worst scores in history, and how much it actually predicts NFL success.
  </p>
</div><div class="article-wrap">
<div class="content">

<h2>What Is Relative Athletic Score?</h2>
<p>Relative Athletic Score — commonly called RAS — is a metric created by analyst Kent Lee Platte that converts NFL combine and pro day measurements into a single score on a scale of <strong>0 to 10</strong>. The key word is <em>relative</em>: every measurement is graded not against all players in the league, but specifically against historical data for players at that same position.</p>
<p>This matters enormously. A 4.55 forty-yard dash is below average for a wide receiver but exceptional for a linebacker. A 6'5" frame is ideal for an offensive tackle but undersized for a center. RAS accounts for these positional differences by comparing each measurement to what's historically been recorded at that specific spot on the field.</p>
<p>A RAS of <strong>10.00</strong> means you posted the best athletic profile ever recorded for your position. A RAS of <strong>5.00</strong> is exactly average for your position historically. A RAS of <strong>0.00</strong> means historically poor athleticism at that spot.</p>
<div class="callout">
<div class="callout-icon">💡</div>
<div class="callout-text"><strong>Who created RAS?</strong> Kent Lee Platte developed RAS and has tracked it publicly on social media and his website since around 2012. It's become a widely referenced tool in draft analytics communities and is cited by major sports media during combine season. The methodology is transparent and the historical database spans decades of combine data.</div>
</div>

<h2>How RAS Is Calculated</h2>
<p>RAS pulls from up to <strong>ten combine and pro day measurements</strong> and organizes them into four categories:</p>
<table class="data-table">
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Category</th>
<th>Measurements Included</th>
<th>What It Captures</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Size</td><td>Height, weight</td><td>Physical frame relative to position norms</td></tr>
<tr><td>Speed</td><td>40-yard dash, 10-yard split, 20-yard split</td><td>Linear speed and acceleration</td></tr>
<tr><td>Explosion</td><td>Vertical jump, broad jump</td><td>Lower body explosive power</td></tr>
<tr><td>Agility</td><td>Three-cone drill, 20-yard shuttle</td><td>Change of direction and quickness</td></tr>
<tr><td>Strength</td><td>225 lb bench press reps</td><td>Upper body strength endurance</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Each individual measurement is converted to a score between 0 and 10 based on where it falls in the historical distribution for that position. Those individual scores are then averaged — weighted by category — into the final composite RAS.</p>
<p><strong>Missing measurements don't automatically tank a score.</strong> If a player skips the bench press, RAS uses only the available data. However, players who skip multiple tests have less reliable scores because fewer data points are available. This is why some analysts prefer to look at individual category scores rather than the composite when key measurements are missing.</p>
<div class="callout">
<div class="callout-icon">⚠️</div>
<div class="callout-text"><strong>Pro day data:</strong> When combine measurements aren't available, RAS incorporates pro day numbers. Pro day conditions are less standardized than the combine — surfaces, timing methods, and conditions vary. Pro day times are generally considered slightly faster than combine times. This introduces some noise into RAS comparisons across players who tested at different venues.</div>
</div>

<h2>The RAS Scale — What Scores Mean</h2>
<div class="ras-scale">
<div class="ras-scale-title">RAS Score Tiers — What Each Range Means</div>
<div class="ras-tier" style="background:rgba(0,207,255,0.08)">
<div class="ras-tier-score" style="color:#00cfff">9.0–10.0</div>
<div class="ras-tier-bar" style="background:#00cfff;width:100%"></div>
