<p>It's worth noting that the distribution of RAS scores is not uniform. <strong>Most NFL draft prospects cluster in the 4.0 to 7.0 range</strong> — the extremes on both ends are relatively rare. A score above 8.5 genuinely represents a rare physical profile at that position.</p>

<h2>RAS by Position — What to Look For</h2>
<p>Each position prioritizes different physical attributes, which means the most important sub-scores within RAS vary by where a player lines up. Here's what matters most and what average and elite RAS looks like at each position.</p>
<div class="position-grid">
<div class="position-card">
<div class="position-card-name">Wide Receiver</div>
<div class="position-card-tag">Speed + Explosion Most Important</div>
<div class="position-card-stats">
<div class="pos-stat">
<div class="pos-stat-val">6.2</div>
<div class="pos-stat-label">Avg RAS</div>
</div>
<div class="pos-stat">
<div class="pos-stat-val">8.5+</div>
<div class="pos-stat-label">Elite RAS</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="position-card-desc">Speed score and explosion score are most predictive for WRs. Agility matters less than raw speed — many elite WRs have below-average three-cone scores. Size is secondary to athleticism at this position.</div>
</div>
<div class="position-card">
<div class="position-card-name">Cornerback</div>
<div class="position-card-tag">Speed + Agility Critical</div>
<div class="position-card-stats">
<div class="pos-stat">
<div class="pos-stat-val">6.4</div>
<div class="pos-stat-label">Avg RAS</div>
</div>
<div class="pos-stat">
<div class="pos-stat-val">8.5+</div>
<div class="pos-stat-label">Elite RAS</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="position-card-desc">CBs need both speed and agility — unlike WRs where pure speed dominates. The three-cone drill is highly predictive for corners. Size is a separate evaluation from RAS — a small CB with a 9.0 RAS faces different NFL questions than a big one.</div>
</div>
<div class="position-card">
<div class="position-card-name">Running Back</div>
<div class="position-card-tag">Explosion + Speed Balance</div>
<div class="position-card-stats">
<div class="pos-stat">
<div class="pos-stat-val">6.1</div>
<div class="pos-stat-label">Avg RAS</div>
</div>
<div class="pos-stat">
<div class="pos-stat-val">8.0+</div>
<div class="pos-stat-label">Elite RAS</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="position-card-desc">Explosion and agility scores carry the most weight for RBs. A high broad jump and short shuttle time suggest a back who can accelerate quickly and cut sharply — the most valuable physical traits at the position.</div>
</div>
<div class="position-card">
<div class="position-card-name">Linebacker</div>
<div class="position-card-tag">Agility + Size Balance</div>
<div class="position-card-stats">
<div class="pos-stat">
<div class="pos-stat-val">5.8</div>
<div class="pos-stat-label">Avg RAS</div>
</div>
<div class="pos-stat">
<div class="pos-stat-val">8.0+</div>
<div class="pos-stat-label">Elite RAS</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="position-card-desc">Linebackers need size within their position range plus agility to cover ground in space. A LB who is large but runs well relative to his size scores extremely high. The three-cone and shuttle are more predictive than the 40 for this group.</div>
</div>
<div class="position-card">
<div class="position-card-name">Offensive Tackle</div>
<div class="position-card-tag">Size + Agility For Frame</div>
<div class="position-card-stats">
<div class="pos-stat">
<div class="pos-stat-val">5.5</div>
<div class="pos-stat-label">Avg RAS</div>
</div>
<div class="pos-stat">
<div class="pos-stat-val">7.5+</div>
<div class="pos-stat-label">Elite RAS</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="position-card-desc">For OTs, size and athleticism relative to that size matter most. A 320-pound tackle who runs a 4.9 forty scores extremely well. The broad jump is predictive for OTs — it correlates with the lateral explosion needed in pass protection.</div>
</div>
<div class="position-card">
<div class="position-card-name">Quarterback</div>
<div class="position-card-tag">Athleticism Context Varies</div>
<div class="position-card-stats">
<div class="pos-stat">
<div class="pos-stat-val">5.2</div>
<div class="pos-stat-label">Avg RAS</div>
</div>
<div class="pos-stat">
<div class="pos-stat-val">7.0+</div>
<div class="pos-stat-label">Athletic QB</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="position-card-desc">RAS is least predictive for QBs because the position is so skill-dependent. Many great QBs have terrible RAS scores. A high QB RAS signals a dual-threat profile — it says almost nothing about accuracy, decision-making, or arm talent.</div>
</div>
