<h2>Does RAS Actually Predict NFL Success?</h2>
<p>This is the most important question — and the answer is nuanced.</p>
<div class="research-box">
<div class="research-label">What the Data Shows</div>
<div class="research-text">Studies on RAS and NFL success show a <strong>modest positive correlation</strong> — meaning high-RAS players on average have slightly better NFL careers than low-RAS players. But the correlation is weak, and the variance is enormous. Many elite NFL players have terrible RAS scores, and many highly-rated athletes have short or unproductive careers.</div>
</div>
<h3>Where RAS Is Most Predictive</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Wide Receiver and Cornerback</strong> — these are the positions where athleticism most directly translates to on-field performance. High-RAS WRs and CBs have meaningfully better career outcomes on average than low-RAS players at the same positions.</li>
<li><strong>Edge Rusher / Defensive End</strong> — speed off the edge translates directly to pass rush production. RAS is a reasonable predictor of pass rush potential.</li>
<li><strong>First-round picks</strong> — among players drafted highly, RAS helps differentiate athletes who were drafted on physical upside from those whose production earned the pick.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Where RAS Is Least Predictive</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Quarterback</strong> — as Tom Brady, Drew Brees, and countless others prove, QB success is overwhelmingly determined by skill, intelligence, and leadership rather than athleticism.</li>
<li><strong>Offensive Line</strong> — technique, football IQ, and hand usage matter far more than athletic testing for linemen. Many great offensive linemen are mediocre athletes by RAS standards.</li>
<li><strong>Late-round picks</strong> — players drafted in rounds 4–7 often succeed through skill, work ethic, and football-specific abilities that RAS doesn't capture.</li>
</ul>

<h2>How to Use RAS as a Draft Tool</h2>
<p>The most sophisticated way to use RAS in draft evaluation is as a <strong>floor indicator rather than a ceiling predictor</strong>. Here's what that means in practice:</p>
<p><strong>High RAS + high production = strong prospect.</strong> When a player has elite athleticism AND elite college production, the combination is genuinely predictive. The physical tools are verified and so is the ability to use them.</p>
<p><strong>High RAS + low production = boom-or-bust.</strong> A physically gifted player who didn't produce in college may have untapped potential — or may simply lack the football skills to translate athleticism into results. These are the players who "look the part" but carry significant risk.</p>
<p><strong>Low RAS + high production = underrated.</strong> Players who beat their athletic profile are often the ones who succeed through superior technique and football intelligence. These prospects are frequently undervalued in draft circles that over-weight combine results.</p>
<p><strong>Low RAS + low production = avoid.</strong> Players with neither athletic tools nor college production have very little going for them at the next level.</p>

