<h2>RAS vs Other Draft Metrics</h2>
<p>RAS is not the only athleticism metric used in NFL draft analysis. Here's how it compares to other commonly referenced tools:</p>
<table class="data-table">
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Metric</th>
<th>What It Measures</th>
<th>Strength</th>
<th>Weakness</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr class="highlight"><td>RAS</td><td>Overall athleticism vs position history</td><td>Positional context, composite view</td><td>Doesn't capture football skill</td></tr>
<tr><td>Speed Score</td><td>40 time adjusted for weight</td><td>Great for skill positions</td><td>Single metric, ignores explosion</td></tr>
<tr><td>SPARQ</td><td>Nike's composite athleticism score</td><td>Used widely at the high school level</td><td>Less common in NFL draft analytics</td></tr>
<tr><td>Burst Score</td><td>Combines vertical and broad jump</td><td>Strong predictor for certain positions</td><td>Ignores speed and agility</td></tr>
<tr><td>Agility Score</td><td>Combines three-cone and shuttle</td><td>Highly predictive for CBs and LBs</td><td>Less relevant for speed positions</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>RAS is generally preferred by analysts because it's the most comprehensive single number that accounts for positional context. Its main limitation — that it says nothing about football skill — is a feature, not a bug. It's designed to answer one specific question: <em>how does this player's physical profile compare to others who have played this position?</em></p>

<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="faq-item">
<div class="faq-q">What is a good RAS score for the NFL draft?</div>
<div class="faq-a">A RAS above 6.5 is above average athleticism for an NFL prospect. Scores above 8.0 are elite. Scores of 9.0 or above are exceptional and represent some of the best physical profiles ever recorded at that position. However, a good RAS alone does not make a good NFL player — college production and football-specific skills matter far more for predicting actual career success.</div>
</div>
<div class="faq-item">
<div class="faq-q">Does RAS matter more for some positions than others?</div>
<div class="faq-a">Yes. RAS is most predictive for wide receivers, cornerbacks, and edge rushers — positions where athleticism directly converts to on-field production. It is least predictive for quarterbacks and offensive linemen, where technique, intelligence, and football-specific skills dominate over raw athleticism. For skill positions going in the first round, RAS is a meaningful data point. For linemen or late-round picks, it's much less relevant.</div>
</div>
<div class="faq-item">
<div class="faq-q">What is Tom Brady's RAS score?</div>
<div class="faq-a">Tom Brady's RAS is approximately 1.0 — one of the lowest ever recorded for a quarterback. His combine included a 5.28 forty-yard dash, poor explosion scores, and an unremarkable physical profile by NFL standards. He was drafted 199th overall in the sixth round of the 2000 draft. His career is the definitive example of why RAS is a measure of physical tools, not of football players.</div>
</div>
<div class="faq-item">
<div class="faq-q">How is RAS different from the NFL combine score?</div>
<div class="faq-a">The NFL combine doesn't produce an official composite score — it produces individual measurements for each test. RAS is a third-party metric created by analyst Kent Lee Platte that converts those individual combine measurements into a single 0-to-10 score by comparing them to historical data at that specific position. Think of RAS as the analytics community's way of summarizing what the raw combine numbers mean in context.</div>
</div>
<div class="faq-item">
<div class="faq-q">Where can I find a player's RAS score?</div>
<div class="faq-a">RAS scores are tracked and published by Kent Lee Platte, who posts them regularly on social media during combine and pro day season. The scores are also referenced by major sports analytics sites and draft publications during the pre-draft period each spring. Searching a player's name along with "RAS" will typically surface their score quickly.</div>
</div>

